2. The Three Research Approaches

Approach A: Serializable / "unserialize()" Reentrancy UAF

Reference article:

https://calif.io/research/php-uaf

Title: Finding and Exploiting a 21-Year-Old Vulnerability in PHP – Calif.

Research background:

The vulnerability involves a missing "BG(serialize_lock)++" operation in "zend_user_unserialize()".

The underlying issue is reentrant deserialization and shared "var_hash" state.

General mechanism:

1. An outer "unserialize()" operation maintains a "var_hash" table containing previously parsed values.
2. A user-defined class implementing "Serializable" invokes another "unserialize()" inside its own "unserialize()" method.
3. The nested operation modifies the inner object's property HashTable.
4. HashTable resizing frees the previous "arData" allocation.
5. References in the outer "var_hash" can still point to freed memory.
6. A subsequent back-reference can access the stale memory.

Research details:

- Uses the "Serializable" interface.
- Involves recursive "unserialize()" calls.
- Exploits stale references in "var_hash".
- The published local exploit demonstrates heap address disclosure, memory read primitives, fake object construction, and a "disable_functions" bypass.
- The article also describes a remote exploitation variant.

Important remote exploitation precondition:

The target application must already load a class that:

- Implements "Serializable".
- Calls "unserialize()" recursively on inner data from its own "unserialize()" method.
- Grows the inner object's property table afterward.

The article explicitly states that this pattern is uncommon in real-world applications.

Important version distinction:

The published article demonstrates a local exploit on PHP 8.5.5. Its claim that the vulnerable code path has existed since PHP 5.1 must not be interpreted as proof that every PHP 8.4.27 build has been independently tested.

Approach B: DateInterval / TimeAfterFree

Reference repository:

https://github.com/m0x41nos/TimeAfterFree

Research background:

This PoC demonstrates a PHP 8 sandbox escape through a UAF vulnerability associated with array destruction and specially constructed objects.

The "DateInterval" internal class is used as an important exploitation component.

General mechanism:

1. Trigger a UAF condition during array/object destruction.
2. Reclaim freed heap memory through controlled allocations.
3. Use "DateInterval" object internals to assist with heap pointer disclosure.
4. Develop memory read/write primitives.
5. Use memory corruption to redirect execution through native PHP/runtime structures.
6. Demonstrate OS command execution despite "disable_functions".

Important distinction:

"DateInterval" is not simply the name of the underlying UAF bug. It is an exploitation primitive/object used by the PoC.

The repository claims compatibility with:

- PHP 8.2.x
- PHP 8.3.x
- PHP 8.4.x
- PHP 8.5.x

It also claims testing across multiple standard PHP distributions and server APIs, including CLI, PHP-FPM, and Apache module.

Investigate whether the published implementation actually works on PHP 8.4.27.

Do not assume that the presence of the built-in "DateInterval" class guarantees exploitability.

Approach C: Stringable / ZEND_FRAMELESS_FUNCTION UAF

Reference article:

https://e1abrador.com/posts/php-uaf-frameless-rce.html

PoC repository:

https://github.com/e1abrador/php-uaf-frameless-rce

Title: PHP 8.4+ UAF via ZEND_FRAMELESS_FUNCTION: From Crash to disable_functions Bypass.

Research background:

PHP 8.4 introduced frameless function execution paths for performance-critical internal functions.

Traditional function calls use VM argument handling that can increment reference counts.

Frameless functions can directly access zval pointers without the same reference-count protection.

General mechanism:

1. A frameless internal function iterates an array.
2. The array contains a custom object implementing "Stringable".
3. The object executes user-defined code through "__toString()".
4. The callback releases the array while the internal function is still iterating it.
5. The array HashTable and its backing allocation can be freed.
6. The internal function continues accessing freed memory.
7. The resulting memory corruption can be turned into a "disable_functions" bypass.

Important technical details:

- The research identifies "preg_replace()" as an exploitable path.
- The PoC uses "Stringable" objects to execute callbacks during array iteration.
- The exploitation chain includes heap pointer disclosure, PHP binary base discovery, fake object construction, and native function dispatch.
- The author discusses ELF symbol resolution and runtime offset discovery.

Important prerequisites reported by the author:

- PHP 8.4 or later.
- OPcache enabled.
- JIT disabled.
- Dynamically linked PHP.
- Read access to "/proc/self/maps".
- Writable "/tmp".

The author reports testing on PHP 8.4.10 and PHP 8.6.0-dev.

The article also discusses fixes applied to "implode()", "strtr()", and "str_replace()", while identifying a similar issue in "preg_replace()".

Investigate the exact status of this issue in PHP 8.4.27.

Do not assume the entire vulnerability class was fixed just because some related functions were patched.

